==County coding==
[[File:Nebraskaplatenumbers.png|right|400px|thumb|Nebraska counties by license plate prefix]]
Nebraska established a county-code system for its passenger and motorcycle plates in 1922, with one- or two-digit codes assigned to each county in order of the number of registered vehicles in the county at that time.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.dmv.nebraska.gov/dvr/plates/plthistory.html |title=History Of Nebraska Passenger Vehicle License Plates |publisher=Nebraska Department of Motor Vehicles |access-date=October 29, 2015 |archive-date=December 27, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151227071418/http://www.dmv.nebraska.gov/dvr/plates/plthistory.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> These codes remained constant through 1950.

For 1951, letter codes were used. One-letter codes were assigned to the first counties whose names began with those letters, while all other counties were assigned two-letter codes consisting of the initial letter and the next available letter in their names (the letters I, O and Q were not used). There were three exceptions: [[Douglas County, Nebraska|Douglas County]], the most populous in the state, was assigned single-letter X to increase capacity; [[Otoe County, Nebraska|Otoe County]] was assigned Z as O was not allowed; and [[Dodge County, Nebraska|Dodge County]] was assigned DG instead of DD as double-letter codes were also not allowed.<ref>{{cite web |last=Reback |first=Brian P. |title=Nebraska - county codes |url=http://www.bprlicenseplates.com/Nebraska%20-%20county%20codes.htm |publisher=bprlicenseplates.com |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150312143912/http://www.bprlicenseplates.com/Nebraska%20-%20county%20codes.htm |archive-date=March 12, 2015 |access-date=March 11, 2016}}</ref>

The numeric code system was reintroduced in 1952, with the codes the same as before. It remains in use, except in Douglas, [[Lancaster County, Nebraska|Lancaster]], and [[Sarpy County, Nebraska|Sarpy]] Counties, which adopted an uncoded ABC 123 serial format in 2002.

